        "description": "Cosmos3 Nano I2V profile for SGLang-Diffusion main 3d540563.",
        "notes": "4-GPU CFG parallel plus Ulysses SP for long-video H200 serving; guardrails disabled, no cache. Policy-aligned candidate (compile on, offload off) \u2014 NOT yet validated on H100; validate (incl. OOM risk) before publishing. FOUR-GPU CELLS ON THE 2026-09-13 4xB200 RUN ARE 3-4x SLOW AND IT IS NOT SGLANG. All three 4-GPU cases (this one, cosmos3 t2v 189f, cosmos3 i2v 189f) came out 3-4x slower than the SAME case and workload on H100 (07-03, 07-30) and H200 (08-26), three runs that agree with each other inside 2%. Every framework in those cells moved together -- sglang 0.25-0.32x, vLLM-Omni 0.28-0.30x, LightX2V 0.22x -- so the earlier note here calling it a suspected sglang regression was wrong, and is retracted. Nor is it the box: every 2-GPU case in that same run is 1.7-2.6x FASTER than its H200 figure. The discriminator is GPU COUNT, not model and not framework; the original investigation missed it because every image case is 2-GPU. Already eliminated, do not repeat: model unchanged since 2025-08-09, workload identical in both artifacts, warmup scales linearly, attention backend (`fa` is WORSE than the auto-selected dynamic_cudnn_sdpa), interconnect topology (NV18 all-pairs at 53.125 GB/s), offload off with 113 GiB free, MoE boundary 0.875 with both experts resident. SETTLED: a fresh 4xB200 does NOT reproduce it. MEASURED 2026-09-18 on a fresh 4xB200 (verda-b200-fin-03-3, same node family, sglang v0.5.20, cosmos3 t2v 189f, identical workload and the same `--attention-backend fa`): 2 GPU on cards 0,1 = 58.05s; 2 GPU on cards 2,3 = 58.24s; 4 GPU = 32.34s. Four-GPU scaling is healthy (1.80x over two), and the 4-GPU figure is 1.64x faster than H200's 53.09s -- the normal Blackwell gain. bench-0912 reported 214.90s for that same cell, 6.6x the healthy number. So the hardware is fine and these three cells are ARTIFACTS OF THAT BOX, not Blackwell and not sglang -- which is also the only account consistent with vLLM-Omni and LightX2V being equally slow there. Two further hypotheses died in the same experiment: `fa` is not pathological on Blackwell (arm A pins it and is 1.9x faster than H200), and the NUMA split that puts GPU3 on a different socket costs nothing measurable (58.05 vs 58.24 across it). RE-MEASURED 2026-09-18 and republished: cosmos3 t2v 214.9 -> 31.7, i2v 216.0 -> 31.9, wan22 669.4 -> 106.5, each now 1.68-1.98x faster than its H200 figure, in line with every 2-GPU cell. The two cosmos3 cells ran against Cosmos3-Nano e59a53c25 rather than the 7a312c868 the rest of the artifact records -- the shared cache resolves `main` online, so the revision could not be pinned; both cells reproduce under 0.1% across two runs and vLLM-Omni moved by the same factor, so the revision does not carry the result.",
